  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "id": "H1",
      "statement": "A-37573 has deep, repeated onward-link sequences, and four of its path accounts sent from one device, D-45880. That fits the hypothesis that one party operates those accounts as a relay. The data does not establish ownership, control, or that the same funds moved. A shared device is consistent with other explanations too, such as a shared or institutional terminal or a data artefact.",
      "references": [
        "F2",
        "F3",
        "sd-45880"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "H2",
      "statement": "A-10859 and A-83577 show the same pattern more weakly: a device shared by two path accounts (D-79802 and D-40018). For D-40018, the presence of the off-route account A-34114 could mean a shared or reused device rather than common control. Both readings are hypotheses only.",
      "references": [
        "F5",
        "F7",
        "F8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "H3",
      "statement": "A-28137, A-85921 and A-20636 have no or shallow onward links and no shared devices on their paths. They could be benign aggregation, for example collections or a merchant-like receiver. The uniform 2500 amounts into A-85921 could be fixed-price payments or structured payments. The data does not say which, and these alerts should not be ranked as innocuous on that basis alone.",
      "references": [
        "F4",
        "F6",
        "F9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "H4",
      "statement": "Ranking by recorded relationships alone, A-37573 looks the most worth reviewing, followed by A-83577 and A-10859. This is a triage suggestion, not a finding of fraud.",
      "references": [
        "F11"
      ]
    }
  ],
